  "summary": "A traditional wax-resist dyeing technique used to pattern textiles, particularly in Java, Indonesia, where it has been developed to a high art form.",
  "description": "Batik creates intricate patterns by applying hot wax to fabric in specific areas before dyeing, preventing the dye from penetrating the waxed sections, resulting in detailed designs with crisp outlines. The technique has deep cultural significance in Indonesia, with certain patterns reserved for royalty and special occasions.",
  "culturalContext": "Batik — Java's wax-resist dyeing (UNESCO Masterpiece, 2009) — writes Javanese life in canting-drawn lines and cap-stamped repeats: parang (blade patterns, once royal monopoly), kawung, sido-mukto wedding motifs, and north-coast (Pekalongan) Chinese- and Dutch-influenced brights. Court batik encoded rank; independence made it national dress (Friday batik mandates); contemporary designers (Iwan Tirta, Danar Hadi archives) keep both court canon and coastal pop alive.",
